From: Fold 3 mainline port
Subject: [PATCH] power: supply: max77705: do not wrap the input limit to
 maximum in the AICL loop

max77705_aicl_irq() walks CHGIN_LIM down one step per 100 ms for as long as
the hardware reports it is still limiting. regval is unsigned and the loop has
no lower bound, so on a supply weak enough to keep AICL asserted all the way
down, the decrement at regval == 0 writes 0xffffffff. regmap_field_write()
masks that to the 7-bit field, giving 0x7f -- the input limit jumps from its
minimum straight to the maximum of 3200 mA, which is the exact opposite of
what the loop is trying to do, and then AICL asserts again and it oscillates.

Observed on a Galaxy Z Fold 3 (q2q), where a weak USB hub port had AICL
actively walking the limit down from 1500 mA within seconds of the driver
binding, so this path is not theoretical.

Stop at the lowest limit the field can express, which is also the value
max77705_get_input_current() reports as MAX77705_CURRENT_CHGIN_MIN.

--- a/drivers/power/supply/max77705_charger.c
+++ b/drivers/power/supply/max77705_charger.c
@@ -57,6 +57,15 @@
 		if (err < 0)
 			return IRQ_HANDLED;
 
+		/*
+		 * Stop at the lowest limit the field can express rather than
+		 * wrapping: CHGIN_LIM is 7 bits, so decrementing past zero
+		 * would write 0x7f and jump the input limit to its maximum --
+		 * the opposite of what this loop is for.
+		 */
+		if (regval <= MAX77705_CURRENT_CHGIN_MIN / MAX77705_CURRENT_CHGIN_STEP - 1)
+			break;
+
 		regval--;
 
 		err = regmap_field_write(chg->rfield[MAX77705_CHG_CHGIN_LIM], regval);
